      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**, which means it is made up of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\n*   **Composition:** It consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n*   **Polarity:** The oxygen atom is slightly \"negative\" and the hydrogen atoms are slightly \"positive.\" This makes water a **polar molecule**. Because of this polarity, water molecules are attracted to one another (cohesion) and to other charged particles (adhesion).\n\n### 2. Key Physical Properties\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Because of its polarity, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it is essential for transporting nutrients and waste in living organisms.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together strongly at the surface, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This is why water forms droplets and why some insects can walk on water.\n*   **States of Matter:** Water is unique because it can exist as a solid (ice), a liquid, and a gas (water vapor) all at the same temperature (0°C to 100°C). This is why ice floats on liquid water; as water freezes, it expands, making it less dense than the liquid form.\n\n### 3. Biological Importance\n*   **Essential for Life:** All known forms of life require water to survive. It acts as a solvent for biochemical reactions, helps regulate body temperature, and provides the medium in which cells function.\n*   **Human Body:** The human body is approximately 60% water. It is found in blood, lymph, and cytoplasm.\n\nIn short, water is the transparent, odorless, tasteless liquid that covers about 71% of the Earth's surface and is the foundation of all known life."
